{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WEUe4gmBEPuUZ1HVkz1zDH","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"title\": null,\n  \"characters_present\": [\n    \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n    \"Clerval\",\n    \"William Frankenstein\",\n    \"Elizabeth\",\n    \"Alphonse Frankenstein\",\n    \"Ernest Frankenstein\",\n    \"Justine Moritz\"\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"After receiving a devastating letter from his father detailing the murder of his younger brother William—whose body was found with the imprint of a murderer’s finger on his neck and with their mother’s miniature portrait missing—Victor Frankenstein departs immediately by cabriolet for Geneva, accompanied by his friend Clerval, who offers only quiet sympathy. En route, he pauses in Lausanne, where the serene beauty of the lake and mountains momentarily soothes his grief before it returns with renewed force. Arriving at Geneva after dark, he cannot enter the city and instead crosses Lake Geneva by boat to reach Plainpalais, the site of the murder; during a violent thunderstorm, he sees the monstrous creature he created standing among the trees, and is certain it is William’s killer. Victor chooses silence, fearing no one would believe his tale of a supernatural being he himself brought to life, and walks to his father’s house just before dawn, where he is met by his brother Ernest, who reveals that Justine Moritz has been accused of the murder after the miniature portrait was found in her possession. Victor insists on Justine’s innocence and knows the true murderer, but again refrains from revealing the creature, fearing he will be deemed insane, while his father and Elizabeth express hope for Justine’s trial, unaware of the truth.\"\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f333195f-8659-4822-8bbf-36772d33b193","uuid":"bea5bb07-775e-4df4-a638-cadfb4daa1e3","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"title\": null,\n  \"characters_present\": [\n    \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n    \"Clerval\",\n    \"William Frankenstein\",\n    \"Elizabeth\",\n    \"Alphonse Frankenstein\",\n    \"Ernest Frankenstein\",\n    \"Justine Moritz\"\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"After receiving a devastating letter from his father detailing the murder of his younger brother William—whose body was found with the imprint of a murderer’s finger on his neck and with their mother’s miniature portrait missing—Victor Frankenstein departs immediately by cabriolet for Geneva, accompanied by his friend Clerval, who offers only quiet sympathy. En route, he pauses in Lausanne, where the serene beauty of the lake and mountains momentarily soothes his grief before it returns with renewed force. Arriving at Geneva after dark, he cannot enter the city and instead crosses Lake Geneva by boat to reach Plainpalais, the site of the murder; during a violent thunderstorm, he sees the monstrous creature he created standing among the trees, and is certain it is William’s killer. Victor chooses silence, fearing no one would believe his tale of a supernatural being he himself brought to life, and walks to his father’s house just before dawn, where he is met by his brother Ernest, who reveals that Justine Moritz has been accused of the murder after the miniature portrait was found in her possession. Victor insists on Justine’s innocence and knows the true murderer, but again refrains from revealing the creature, fearing he will be deemed insane, while his father and Elizabeth express hope for Justine’s trial, unaware of the truth.\"\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
